      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "More than force",
      "body": "Rome held its empire together with roads, a shared army, citizenship, law and trade, not by force alone. Local elites ran their own cities, and in 212 CE almost every free person became a citizen."
    },
    {
      "id": "card.ancient_rome.080.c3",
      "type": "text",
      "role": "explain",
      "headline": "Becoming Roman",
      "body": "Historians call the spread of Roman language, culture and ways of life in the provinces Romanization. Towns across the West built forums, baths and amphitheaters on the Roman model. Latin spread across the West with soldiers, settlers and officials.",
      "callout": "Romanization: provinces adopting Roman ways"
    },
